    "https://career5.successfactors.eu/career?career%5fns=job%5flisting&company=S003974031P&navBarLevel=JOB%5fSEARCH&rcm%5fsite%5flocale=nl%5fNL&career_job_req_id=24478&selected_lang=nl_NL&jobAlertController_jobAlertId=&jobAlertController_jobAlertName=&browserTimeZone=America/Los_Angeles&_s.crb=%2bjNT0fgglEEWcUKC1NsSnNQSe6oMt6N1AhRiVmfcgZ8%3d": {
      "title": "Scientific research assistant Cognitive Neuropsychology",
      "description": "Scientific research assistant Cognitive Neuropsychology (24478) JavaScript is uitgeschakeld in de webbrowser. Schakel JavaScript in om optimaal te profiteren van deze site, en vernieuw de pagina vervolgens. Laden... Verdergaan naar primaire inhoud Scientific research assistant Cognitive Neuropsychology (24478) Vacature ID 24478 - Gepubliceerd - Vacatures voor wetenschappelijk personeel - Tilburg School of Social and Behavioral  Sciences  Afdrukvoorbeeld vacatureomschrijving Solliciteren Vacature opslaan Vacature naar een vriend sturen Terug naar lijst Tilburg University | Tilburg School of Social and Behavioral Sciences is looking for a Scientific research assistant Department: Cognitive Neuropsychology On site: Tilburg Contract size: 0.6 fte (24 hours per week) Full-time gross monthly salary: max € 3.369 ,- Contract duration: 12 months Desired starting date: November 1, 2026 Are you eager to dive into cutting-edge neuromodulation and real-world symptom monitoring? As a research assistant in our team, you will be running TMS sessions, working with intensive longitudinal data from daily life, and contributing to a new, state-informed approach to brain stimulation. This position offers the unique opportunity to work at the intersection of neuroscience, data science, and clinical research, with room for creativity, technical exploration, and meaningful impact. Here you'll have the chance to help deliver an iTBS protocol targeting the left prefrontal cortex, to model how cognitive fatigue fluctuates from moment to moment, and to explore who responds to stimulation and when. Curious minds are very welcome. Context In this project, members of the Cognitive Neuropsychology Department at Tilburg University aim to bridge real-time symptom monitoring in daily life with a pilot neuromodulation design, to better understand when and for whom stimulation may be most effective. By identifying dynamic fatigue profiles in students with elevated fatigue and relating them to short-term response to iTBS, the project lays the groundwork for more personalized, state-informed brain stimulation in clinical populations experiencing \"mental fog\". Your position As a Research Assistant, you will support an ongoing pilot project that combines ecological momentary assessment (EMA) with transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS), with the broader goal of understanding cognitive fatigue as a dynamic process. Your work will involve a short literature study, hands-on involvement in data collection (EMA via a smartphone app and iTBS sessions in the lab), and initial support of the data analysis. Over time, you'll help document the workflows that link daily-life symptom dynamics to short-term response to stimulation. You'll mainly focus on the data-collection (recruitment, running TMS sessions, monitoring EMA compliance) and later on explore preliminary analyses (processing intensive longitudinal data). You'll be actively involved in: Recruiting and scheduling participants and running EMA and iTBS sessions Following predefined TMS procedures (resting motor threshold, Beam F3 localization, delivering the iTBS protocol) under supervision Monitoring and managing the EMA data collection and participant compliance Contributing to the processing and analysis of intensive longitudinal (EMA) and stimulation data Summarizing results and documenting workflows, and collaborating with the research team on interim findings and next steps This way, you'll have an influence on the direction of the project and the opportunity to apply and further refine valuable skills at the intersection of neuromodulation, data science, and clinical research. What your typical week might look like: You'll split your time between the lab and independent work, i.e. running participant sessions (TMS and EMA onboarding), meeting with supervisors to discuss progress and troubleshoot technical or practical challenges, and documenting your workflow and results. Some weeks may be focused on data collection and participant contact, others on managing and interpreting the EMA and stimulation data, or preparing summary reports. Your profile You are a motivated recent graduate, with a strong analytical mindset and an interest in neuromodulation, symptom dynamics, and technological innovation in clinical care. We are looking for someone who meets the following criteria: You recently completed a bachelor’s or master's degree in (neuro-)psychology, social or biomedical sciences, (bio-)engineering, or a related field. You have demonstrable affinity with neuroscience and/or human research (e.g. through coursework, your thesis, internship, or previous research projects). You are comfortable working with statistical data analysis software or very motivated to learn new tools (e.g. R, STATA, or alternative coding). Scientific and/or practical experience with neuroscientific methods such as neuromodulation (TMS) and/or intensive longitudinal/EMA data is not required but is an asset. You are eager to take initiative, enjoy solving methodological and practical challenges. You collaborate well but can also work independently when running sessions or exploring analyses. You are reliable and comfortable in participant-facing tasks, and you handle sensitive data with care. You have sufficient proficiency in English to read scientific literature, communicate in a research context, and document your work clearly; proficiency in Dutch is an asset for participant contact. Competencies: Organizing ability: you can coordinate and keep track of a multimodal data collection (participant scheduling, TMS sessions, and continuous EMA sampling running in parallel), managing the practical logistics. Initiative: you take ownership of tasks, identify opportunities for improvement, and follow through independently. Accuracy: you pay attention to detail and ensure the quality and traceability of your work. Collaboration: you can communicate effectively within a research team and contribute constructively to shared goals. Our offer Tilburg University offers excellent benefits in a pleasant working environment: Employment of 0.6 fte(24hours per week), depending om the duration of the contract. A salary of € 3.369 gross per month for full-time employment, (UFO profile research assistant, salary scale 7 step 2). Tilburg University uses a neutral remuneration system based on relevant work experience. This is a vacancy for a position in accordance with Article 2.3 paragraph 1 CLA DU. You will be given a temporary contract for the duration of 12 months. Vacation pay of 8% and a year-end bonus of 8.3%. Over 8 weeks of vacation leave. Hybrid working with home working allowance (€2 p/day) and internet allowance (€25 p/month). Reimbursement for sustainable commuting: walking, cycling, and public transport. Options model for additional leave, pension, a bicycle or personal training. Pension through ABP. Ample training offer: personal development, leadership, education, research or language courses. Inclusive work culture: everyone welcome, equal opportunities. A green, inviting campus that’s easily accessible by public transport. For more information, see our website and the CLA Dutch Universities . Want to know more? Would you like to know more before applying? Feel free to contact Charlotte Sleurs at 0032476389401 or c.sleurs@tilburguniversity.edu . Want to apply? We kindly invite you to apply before September 27, 2026 ; this can only be done online . Address your cover letter to Charlotte Sleurs and attach your resume. Letter selection is scheduled on 28/09/26; you will receive notification from us shortly thereafter. The first selection interviews will take place on Sep 30th and the second on Oct 6th. The selection committee consists of: Charlotte Sleurs, Assistant Professor Dept. Cognitive Neuropsychology Dagmar Timmers, PhD, Dept. Cognitive Neuropsychology You will ideally start working for Tilburg University on 01/11/2026. Over Tilburg University Tilburg University is dé universiteit voor mens- en maatschappijwetenschappen. Al sinds onze oprichting in 1927 zetten we ons in voor een rechtvaardige, inclusieve en duurzame samenleving. Met toonaangevend onderzoek en inspirerend onderwijs dragen wij bij aan duiding en waar mogelijk aan oplossingen voor actuele maatschappelijke vraagstukken, van klimaatverandering tot digitalisering Onze gemeenschap is persoonlijk, betrokken en kleinschalig. Op onze groene, compacte en levendige campus is ruimte voor ontmoeting, samenwerking én reflectie en persoonlijke groei. Lees meer over Tilburg University . Werken bij Tilburg University doe je tegen aantrekkelijke arbeidsvoorwaarden in een inclusief team. Hier krijg je volop kansen voor het versterken van je talenten en het ontwikkelen van een loopbaan. In een warme werksfeer waarin we hechten aan de waarden: curious, connected, caring en courageous. Over Tilburg School of Social and Behavioral Sciences (TSB) Met ons onderwijs en onderzoek verdiepen we ons in menselijk gedrag in een veranderende samenleving. Bij Tilburg School of Social and Behavioral Sciences (TSB) willen we begrijpen hoe mensen functioneren als individuen, in relaties, in organisaties en in de samenleving. We willen aanpassingsvermogen, mentale veerkracht en grip op gezondheid en welbevinden versterken. Bij TSB werken onderzoekers steeds meer in interdisciplinaire teams die de krachten van alle sociaalwetenschappelijke disciplines bundelen om onderzoeksvragen te beantwoorden. Daarnaast werken zij samen met burgers, professionals, beleidsmakers, bestuurders en ondernemers om actuele maatschappelijke problemen te bestuderen en op te lossen. Docenten bij TSB dragen bij aan het ontwerpen en uitvoeren van opleidingen in digitale sociale wetenschappen die studenten de gelegenheid bieden vakken te volgen uit een breed scala aan disciplines. Lees meer over de Tilburg School of Social and Behavioral Sciences . Sollicitatiecode Tilburg University hanteert de NVP-sollicitatiecode van de Nederlandse Vereniging voor Personeelsmanagement & Organisatieontwikkeling. Disclaimer De tekst in deze vacatureadvertentie is auteursrechtelijk beschermd eigendom van Tilburg University. Gebruik, verspreiding en verdere openbaarmaking van de advertentie zonder uitdrukkelijke toestemming van Tilburg University is niet toegestaan en dat geldt expliciet voor gebruik door werving- en selectiebureaus welke niet rechtstreeks handelen in opdracht van de Tilburg University. Reacties naar aanleiding van werving door niet-opdrachtnemers van Tilburg University worden niet in behandeling genomen. Solliciteren Vacature opslaan Vacature naar een vriend sturen Terug naar lijst Deze functie naar een vriend sturen   De functie is verstuurd naar Geef onderstaande informatie op Vacaturetitel: * E-mailadres van uw vriend: Bericht: Maximum aantal tekens: 1000 * Bevestig dat u geen robot bent: Verzenden Annuleren"
    }
  },
